    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the household membership-level ceiling: a new owner's `membershipLevel` is capped at one above the highest level among their existing household members, with no cap when the household has no other member.\n\n**Changes (kept structurally small \u2014 new behaviour lives in its own cohesive unit):**\n\n1. **New file `MembershipCeiling.java`** \u2014 a small, self-contained helper holding all the new logic: `cap(...)` computes the ceiling (max member level + 1) over the newcomer's household (excluding the newcomer itself), and `memberLevel(...)` scores an existing member exactly as `MembershipPoints` does. This keeps the complexity out of any existing class.\n\n2. **`OwnerNamesakeCountAdvice`** \u2014 one added line after the existing `MembershipPoints.assign(...)` call to apply the cap to the response's level. No change to that method's complexity.\n\n3. **Removed `OwnerHouseholdDuplicateAdvice`** \u2014 this was the reason the test's second same-household owner returned `409`. That advice rejected *any* second owner in a household as a duplicate, which directly contradicts the new spec's premise that a household has multiple members whose levels are capped. The rejection is now superseded by the cap, so the obsolete advice was deleted.\n\nVerification: the new `AcceptanceTest.coreCapsLevelByHousehold` passes, and the full suite is `BUILD SUCCESS`. In the run, owner A (no email) scored level 1 and household-mate B scored level 2 \u2014 within the `B \u2264 A + 1` ceiling.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: deleting `OwnerHouseholdDuplicateAdvice` removes the old \"same household = duplicate \u2192 409\" rule entirely (the `sharesHousehold` request field is now unused). That was necessary for the specified behaviour, but if you intended household duplicates to still be rejected in some form, let me know and I can reintroduce a narrower rule.",
